<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wassoulou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wassoulou is a savanna region on the borders of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, around the upper Sankarani River. Its people, largely of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> origin but speaking the Maninka language, gave their name to the Wassoulou Empire founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a> in the late 19th century. The region is also known for its musical tradition, the source of Wassoulou music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassoulou.jpg" alt="Wassoulou" width="282" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T L Miles at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wassoulou_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
